<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Selinous</h1><p class="st-meta">628 BCE – 409 BCE (approx.) · capital Selinous</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The westernmost Greek city in Sicily, famed for its great temples, which was destroyed by Carthage in 409 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Colonists from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/megara-hyblaea">Megara Hyblaea</a>, led by Pammilus, founded Selinous around 628 BCE on a coastal plateau between two rivers in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>; some ancient sources give an earlier date. The name came from wild celery (selinon), which appears on its coins.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Selinous grew rich from its grain-growing lands and trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elymians">Elymians</a>, and it built more large temples than almost any other Greek city: on its acropolis and on the eastern hill stand the remains of at least eight, including the colossal Temple G, and their sculpted metopes are now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>. It sided with Carthage in 480 BCE but later quarrelled over borders with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/segesta">Segesta</a>, which called in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> and Carthage.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 409 BCE a Carthaginian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal-mago">Hannibal Mago</a> stormed Selinous after nine days, killing thousands. The city revived under Carthaginian control, but in 250 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> moved its inhabitants to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lilybaeum">Lilybaeum</a> and abandoned it. Its archaeological park is one of the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 628 BCE – 409 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:47.20%;width:4.36%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>628 BCE – 409 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Selinous</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Selinunte, Selinus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-518&amp;focus=selinous" aria-label="Selinous on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4941 576 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Selinous lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5259 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5261 832h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5261 832h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Italy. <a href="/atlas?year=-518&amp;focus=selinous">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

